# Read by GitHub ("Cite this repository") and by Zenodo on every GitHub
# release. Zenodo maps only title, authors, abstract, keywords and message into
# the archived record -- `abstract` becomes the record's description -- and
# ignores preferred-citation, so the software authors below stay the Zenodo
# creators while GitHub shows the paper. `license` is left out on purpose:
# Zenodo then takes it from the repository's LICENSE (Apache 2.0). An invalid
# file makes Zenodo's import fail, so validate after editing:
# `cffconvert --validate`.
#
# No `version` or `date-released`: Zenodo stamps each release from its tag, and
# `__version__` in genotools/__init__.py stays the only place a release bumps.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use GenoTools, please cite the G3 paper below. To cite the software itself, use the Zenodo concept DOI, which always resolves to the latest release."
type: software
title: "GenoTools"
abstract: >-
  GenoTools is a Python pipeline for genotype data quality control and
  analysis, built on PLINK. It runs sample QC (call rate, sex,
  heterozygosity, relatedness) and variant QC (missingness, case-control and
  haplotype missingness, Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, LD pruning), predicts
  genetic ancestry with a model trained on a reference panel, and runs PCA and
  association testing. It accepts PLINK 1.9 or 2 files or VCF, and every run
  writes a JSON report of what each step removed and why. GenoTools was
  developed as the core genotype processing pipeline for the Global
  Parkinson's Genetics Program (GP2).
